cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or methodology, please cite the preprint below."
title: "Mechanistic Interpretability of EEG Foundation Models via Sparse Autoencoders"
abstract: >-
  EEG foundation models achieve state-of-the-art clinical performance, yet
  the internal computations driving their predictions remain opaque. We apply
  TopK Sparse Autoencoders across three architecturally distinct EEG
  transformers (SleepFM, REVE, LaBraM) to extract sparse feature dictionaries
  from their embeddings, ground these features in a clinical taxonomy
  (abnormality, age, sex, medication), and benchmark monosemanticity and
  entanglement across architectures. Via concept steering with a
  target-vs-off-target selectivity metric, we reveal three operational
  regimes (selectively steerable, encoded-but-entangled, non-encoded) and
  surface "wrecking-ball" interventions and clinical entanglements such as
  age-pathology confounding. A spectral decoder translates latent
  interventions back to amplitude spectra, providing physiologically
  interpretable mechanistic explanations.
